Q: In aarch32 mode, will the values ​of x15 - x30 and the high 32 bits of x0 - x14 affect the program? A: <p>Looking at version M.a.a of <a href="https://developer.arm.com/documentation/ddi0487/latest" rel="nofollow noreferrer">the manual</a>, we can see the following in pseudocode under J1.2.3.70, J1.2.3.74 and J1.2.3.64:</p>
<pre><code>// R - setter
// ==========
R[integer n] = bits(32) value
    Rmode[n, PSTATE.M] = value;
    return;

// R - getter
// ==========
bits(32) R[integer n]
    if n == 15 then
        offset = (if CurrentInstrSet() == InstrSet_A32 then 8 else 4);
        return _PC&lt;31:0&gt; + offset;
    else
        return Rmode[n, PSTATE.M];
</code></pre>
<pre><code>// Rmode - getter
// ==============
bits(32) Rmode[integer n, bits(5) mode]
    assert n &gt;= 0 &amp;&amp; n &lt;= 14;

    // Check for attempted use of Monitor mode in Non-secure state.
    if CurrentSecurityState() != SS_Secure then assert mode != M32_Monitor;
    assert !BadMode(mode);

    if mode == M32_Monitor then
        if n == 13 then return SP_mon;
        elsif n == 14 then return LR_mon;
        else return _R[n]&lt;31:0&gt;;
    else
        return _R[LookUpRIndex(n, mode)]&lt;31:0&gt;;

// Rmode - setter
// ==============
Rmode[integer n, bits(5) mode] = bits(32) value
    assert n &gt;= 0 &amp;&amp; n &lt;= 14;

    // Check for attempted use of Monitor mode in Non-secure state.
    if CurrentSecurityState() != SS_Secure then assert mode != M32_Monitor;
    assert !BadMode(mode);

    if mode == M32_Monitor then
        if n == 13 then SP_mon = value;
        elsif n == 14 then LR_mon = value;
        else _R[n]&lt;31:0&gt; = value;
    else
        // It is CONSTRAINED UNPREDICTABLE whether the upper 32 bits of the X
        // register are unchanged or set to zero. This is also tested for on
        // exception entry, as this applies to all AArch32 registers.
        if HaveAArch64() &amp;&amp; ConstrainUnpredictableBool(Unpredictable_ZEROUPPER) then
            _R[LookUpRIndex(n, mode)] = ZeroExtend(value, 64);
        else
            _R[LookUpRIndex(n, mode)]&lt;31:0&gt; = value;

    return;
</code></pre>
<pre><code>// LookUpRIndex()
// ==============
integer LookUpRIndex(integer n, bits(5) mode)
    assert n &gt;= 0 &amp;&amp; n &lt;= 14;

    integer result;
    case n of // Select index by mode:    usr fiq irq svc abt und hyp
        when 8  result = RBankSelect(mode,  8, 24,  8,  8,  8,  8,  8);
        when 9  result = RBankSelect(mode,  9, 25,  9,  9,  9,  9,  9);
        when 10 result = RBankSelect(mode, 10, 26, 10, 10, 10, 10, 10);
        when 11 result = RBankSelect(mode, 11, 27, 11, 11, 11, 11, 11);
        when 12 result = RBankSelect(mode, 12, 28, 12, 12, 12, 12, 12);
        when 13 result = RBankSelect(mode, 13, 29, 17, 19, 21, 23, 15);
        when 14 result = RBankSelect(mode, 14, 30, 16, 18, 20, 22, 14);
        otherwise result = n;

    return result;
</code></pre>
<p>So as you can see, direct register accesses from usermode are constrained to the bounds <code>n &gt;= 0 &amp;&amp; n &lt;= 14</code>, with <code>15</code> being special-cased to <code>PC</code>. So the registers <code>x15</code> through <code>x30</code> should be safe from direct accesses.</p>
<p>But as that one comment block notes, it is <code>CONSTRAINED UNPREDICTABLE</code> whether register writes zero the upper 32 bits or not, so at the very least the upper 32 bits of <code>x0</code> through <code>x14</code> are not safe.</p>
<p>But what's more, even if registers aren't directly written to by code, they may still be truncated by the architecture. From J1.1.3.243:</p>
<pre><code>// AArch64.MaybeZeroRegisterUppers()
// =================================
// On taking an exception to AArch64 from AArch32, it is CONSTRAINED UNPREDICTABLE whether the top
// 32 bits of registers visible at any lower Exception level using AArch32 are set to zero.
AArch64.MaybeZeroRegisterUppers()
    assert UsingAArch32(); // Always called from AArch32 state before entering AArch64 state

    integer first;
    integer last;
    boolean include_R15;
    if PSTATE.EL == EL0 &amp;&amp; !ELUsingAArch32(EL1) then
        first = 0; last = 14; include_R15 = FALSE;
    elsif PSTATE.EL IN {EL0, EL1} &amp;&amp; EL2Enabled() &amp;&amp; !ELUsingAArch32(EL2) then
        first = 0; last = 30; include_R15 = FALSE;
    else
        first = 0; last = 30; include_R15 = TRUE;

    for n = first to last
        if (n != 15 || include_R15) &amp;&amp; ConstrainUnpredictableBool(Unpredictable_ZEROUPPER) then
            _R[n]&lt;63:32&gt; = Zeros(32);

    return;
</code></pre>
<p>But at least that code gives some reassurance that even ARM considers the registers above <code>x14</code> not &quot;visible&quot; to EL0 AArch32 state, so long as all exception levels above it are using AArch64.</p>

Q: How to scope Tailwind CSS build in a Micro Frontend to prevent CSS bleeding (Host vs. MFE) without Shadow DOM? A: <p>I am actually facing the same issue right now, where I have a host app (a plain html app) that can load X number of Tailwind/React apps on the page as &quot;widgets&quot; and other apps as well.</p>
<p>I am using Tailwind V4 and don't want my theme variables and Tailwind reset in each app leaking to the global CSS. I couldn't find any good documentation on this. I too don't want to prefix my classes with #app-1 or #app-2 because it's messy and forces teams to write their utilities this way, yuck!</p>
<p>I don't have a permanent solution but in the meantime, i've created a plugin that scopes each app for you.</p>
<pre><code>export default function postcssScope(appRootSelector) {
  return {
    postcssPlugin: 'postcss-scope',

    Rule(rule) {
      // Skip if no selector (e.g., @rules without selectors)
      if (!rule.selector) return;

      // Skip if already scoped to this app root
      if (rule.selector.includes(appRootSelector)) return;

      const trimmedSelector = rule.selector.trim();

      // ============================================================
      // STEP 1: Transform :root to app root (for CSS variables)
      // ============================================================
      // Tailwind's @theme outputs variables on :root, which causes conflicts
      // when multiple apps define the same variables. Transform to app root.
      if (isRootSelector(trimmedSelector)) {
        rule.selector = appRootSelector;
        return;
      }

      // ============================================================
      // STEP 2: Handle .dark selector (dark mode)
      // ============================================================
      // .dark is applied to body/html, not inside app root
      // Transform .dark to body.dark #app2-root so dark mode works
      if (trimmedSelector === '.dark') {
        rule.selector = `body.dark ${appRootSelector}, html.dark ${appRootSelector}`;
        return;
      }

      // ============================================================
      // STEP 3: Skip selectors that are already handled
      // ============================================================
      // Skip complex selectors that include :root in combinations
      // (these are already transformed in STEP 1)
      if (trimmedSelector.includes(':root,') || trimmedSelector.includes(', :root')) {
        return;
      }

      // ============================================================
      // STEP 4: Transform html, body in @layer base to app root
      // ============================================================
      // When html, body is in @layer base, transform it to app root
      // This ensures fonts and base styles apply to the app container
      // instead of trying to scope html/body (which won't work)
      if (isHtmlBodyInBaseLayer(rule, trimmedSelector)) {
        rule.selector = appRootSelector;
        return;
      }

      // ============================================================
      // STEP 5: Scope base styles (preflight/reset)
      // ============================================================
      // Scope *, html, body selectors to prevent Tailwind's base styles
      // from affecting other apps on the same page
      // This includes compound selectors like &quot;*,:before,:after&quot;
      if (isBaseStyleSelector(trimmedSelector)) {
        rule.selector = scopeBaseStyle(appRootSelector, trimmedSelector);
        return;
      }

      // ============================================================
      // STEP 6: Scope selectors inside @media and @supports
      // ============================================================
      // @media and @supports can contain scoped selectors, so we still scope them
      // But @keyframes, etc. have their own mechanisms
      if (isInsideSpecialAtRule(rule)) {
        // Scope selectors inside @media and @supports
        if (isInsideMediaOrSupports(rule)) {
          rule.selector = `${appRootSelector} ${rule.selector}`;
        }
        return;
      }

      // ============================================================
      // STEP 7: Scope everything else (utility classes, etc.)
      // ============================================================
      // All remaining selectors (like .flex, .p-4, etc.) get prefixed
      // with the app root selector
      rule.selector = `${appRootSelector} ${rule.selector}`;
    },
  };
}

postcssScope.postcss = true;

// ============================================================
// Helper Functions
// ============================================================

/**
 * Check if selector is a root selector (:root, :host, etc.)
 */
function isRootSelector(selector) {
  return (
    selector === ':root' ||
    selector === ':root,:host' ||
    selector === ':root, :host' ||
    selector.startsWith(':root,') ||
    selector === ':host'
  );
}

/**
 * Check if selector is html, body (or combination) inside @layer base
 * These should be transformed to app root instead of scoped
 */
function isHtmlBodyInBaseLayer(rule, selector) {
  // Check if rule is inside @layer base
  let parent = rule.parent;
  while (parent) {
    if (parent.type === 'atrule' &amp;&amp; parent.name === 'layer' &amp;&amp; parent.params === 'base') {
      // Check if selector is html, body, or combination
      const normalized = selector.toLowerCase().replace(/\s+/g, '');
      return normalized === 'html,body' || normalized === 'html, body' || 
             normalized === 'html' || normalized === 'body';
    }
    parent = parent.parent;
  }
  return false;
}

/**
 * Check if selector is a base style selector (preflight/reset)
 * This includes *, html, body, and compound selectors like &quot;*,:before,:after&quot;
 */
function isBaseStyleSelector(selector) {
  // Check for exact match or if selector starts with *, html, or body
  if (selector === '*' || selector.startsWith('html') || selector.startsWith('body')) {
    return true;
  }

  // Check for compound selectors that start with * (e.g., &quot;*,:before,:after&quot;)
  if (selector.includes(',')) {
    const firstPart = selector.split(',')[0].trim();
    return firstPart === '*' || firstPart.startsWith('html') || firstPart.startsWith('body');
  }

  return false;
}

/**
 * Scope base style selectors to app root
 * Handles both simple (*) and compound selectors (*,:before,:after)
 */
function scopeBaseStyle(appRootSelector, selector) {
  if (selector === '*') {
    return `${appRootSelector} *`;
  }

  // Handle compound selectors (e.g., &quot;*,:before,:after&quot; -&gt; &quot;#app2-root *, #app2-root :before, #app2-root :after&quot;)
  if (selector.includes(',')) {
    return selector
      .split(',')
      .map(part =&gt; {
        const trimmed = part.trim();
        if (trimmed === '*' || trimmed.startsWith('html') || trimmed.startsWith('body')) {
          return `${appRootSelector} ${trimmed}`;
        }
        return trimmed;
      })
      .join(', ');
  }

  // Keep the html/body in the selector for specificity
  // e.g., &quot;html&quot; -&gt; &quot;#app2-root html&quot;
  return `${appRootSelector} ${selector}`;
}

/**
 * Check if rule is inside @media or @supports at-rule
 * These can contain scoped selectors that need to be prefixed
 */
function isInsideMediaOrSupports(rule) {
  if (!rule.parent || rule.parent.type !== 'atrule') return false;
  return rule.parent.name === 'media' || rule.parent.name === 'supports';
}

/**
 * Check if rule is inside a special @rule that should be skipped
 */
function isInsideSpecialAtRule(rule) {
  if (!rule.parent || rule.parent.type !== 'atrule') return false;

  const atRule = rule.parent;
  const specialAtRules = [
    'import',
    'charset',
    'keyframes',
    'container',
  ];

  return specialAtRules.includes(atRule.name);
}
</code></pre>
<p>I'm using a vite bundler:</p>
<pre><code>// vite.config.js
import postcssScope from './postcss-scope.js'

export default defineConfig({
  plugins: [
    tailwindcss(),
  ],
  css: {
    postcss: {
      plugins: [
        postcssScope('#tailwindapp-1'), &lt;-- this is a div wrapping your app
      ],
    },
  },
})
</code></pre>

Q: Input losing focus when rerendering A: <p>Your input is losing focus because you’re <em>destroying/recreating DOM</em> on every keystroke.</p>
<p>Two things in your code make that happen:</p>
<ol>
<li><p>onChange → this.props.editor.forceUpdate()</p>
<p>That forces a re-render of a bigger part of the tree than necessary.</p>
</li>
<li><p>componentDidUpdate does this <strong>every render</strong>:</p>
</li>
</ol>
<pre><code>$(this.getDOMNode()).slimScroll({destroy: true}).slimScroll(...)
</code></pre>
<p>Many jQuery “scroll” plugins implement destroy/re-init by <strong>re-wrapping / replacing nodes</strong>. When the DOM node containing the &lt;input&gt; gets replaced, the browser blurs it → focus is lost.</p>
<h3><strong>Fix (recommended): don’t destroy/re-init slimScroll on every update</strong></h3>
<p>Initialize slimScroll once (mount), and update it without destroying, or only re-init when the <em>height</em> changes (or some scroll-related prop changes), not when typing.</p>
<pre><code>var EditorContainer = React.createClass({
  componentDidMount: function () {
    $(this.getDOMNode()).slimScroll({ height: this.props.height, distance: '4px', size: '8px' });
  },

  componentDidUpdate: function (prevProps) {
    // Only touch slimScroll when relevant props change
    if (prevProps.height !== this.props.height) {
      $(this.getDOMNode())
        .slimScroll({ destroy: true })
        .slimScroll({ height: this.props.height, distance: '4px', size: '8px' });
    }
  },

  changeSelectedComponentName: function (e) {
    // Update data properly (state/store) instead of forceUpdate.
    // Example:
    this.props.editor.selectedComponent.name = e.target.value; // ideally don’t mutate like this
    this.props.editor.forceUpdate(); // still not ideal, but at least focus won't be killed by slimScroll reinit
  },

  render: function () {
    ...
  }
});
</code></pre>
<p>Even better: <strong>don’t call forceUpdate() at all</strong>. Put the input value in component state (or in your store) and update it normally.</p>
<hr />
<h3><strong>If you absolutely must keep the destroy/re-init: re-focus via a ref</strong></h3>
<p>This is a workaround, not the real fix.</p>
<pre><code>var EditorContainer = React.createClass({
  componentDidMount() {
    this.inputEl &amp;&amp; this.inputEl.focus();
    $(this.getDOMNode()).slimScroll({ height: this.props.height, distance: '4px', size: '8px' });
  },

  componentDidUpdate() {
    $(this.getDOMNode()).slimScroll({ destroy: true }).slimScroll({ height: 'auto', distance: '4px', size: '8px' });

    // restore focus
    if (this.inputEl) this.inputEl.focus();
  },

  render() {
    return (
      &lt;input
        ref={(el) =&gt; (this.inputEl = el)}
        type=&quot;text&quot;
        value={this.props.selected ? this.props.selected.name : ''}
        onChange={this.changeSelectedComponentName}
      /&gt;
    );
  }
});
</code></pre>
<p>But again: the real issue is the jQuery plugin re-creating DOM on update. If you stop doing that during typing, the focus problem disappears cleanly.</p>
